  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Sugar, Spice, and the Not So Nice shows early signs of scholarly and educational use: it has 2,240 recorded downloads, is openly available in hybrid form, and is aligned with the Sustainable Development Goal on gender equality. The citation record is still at zero, so the clearest evidence here is readership and reach rather than measured scholarly uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2023, Sugar, Spice, and the Not So Nice is a book-length contribution to comics studies that focuses on girlhood in comics across historical, narratological, aesthetic, and practice-based perspectives. Its subject matter connects directly to gender equality, and the record suggests that this framing has made the book relevant beyond a narrow specialist audience.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is use: the book has been downloaded 2,240 times through OAPEN reporting. That pattern indicates sustained reader interest rather than isolated attention, and the monthly record shows the book drawing activity across the year. Because downloads measure access and consultation, they should be read as evidence of readership, not as a substitute for citation impact.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The work is also available open access in hybrid form, which lowers barriers to discovery and helps explain its reach. Open availability matters especially for a book engaging with girlhood, comics, and marginalised identities, since those themes often intersect with teaching, research, and public-facing discussion in visual culture and gender studies.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the scholarly citation record is still undeveloped in the available data, with zero citations recorded in OpenAlex. That means the book’s longer-term academic influence has not yet accumulated in a measurable way, or at least not in the sources currently indexed here. The evidence at present is therefore one of promising uptake and circulation, with scholarly citation impact still early.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Additional signals point to broader institutional relevance: library holdings are recorded in WorldCat, and the book appears in a peer-review workflow context, which supports its credibility within academic publishing. Overall, the record shows a clearly positioned scholarly book with visible readership, open access reach, and topical alignment with gender equality, while its citation footprint remains limited in the current evidence base.</p>",
